{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>See now that I, I am He.</b> He doubled the saying 'אֲנִי אֲנִי' to exclude the opinion of those who say there are two authorities, one ruling over the good things and one ruling over the evil things. And so Ḥannah said (1 Samuel 2:3): ״אַל תַּרְבּוּ תְדַבְּרוּ גְּבֹהָה גְּבֹהָה״, to say that there are two 'exalted ones,' as mentioned — rather, everything proceeded from a single source: ״HaShem brings low and raises up,״ ״HaShem brings death and gives life״ (there v. 6), for He, blessed be He, brings about all opposites. And Rabbi Moses of Coucy explained, at the beginning of the positive commandments, in the name of Rav Saadya, to answer the heretics who say there are two authorities: if both are free to do each whatever he wishes, and the one sought to give life to a man and the other sought to kill him, that man ought to be dead-alive, alive-dead, in an instant, in the blink of an eye, etc. Therefore He said here: ״אֲנִי אֲנִי הוּא״ — ruling over the good things and over the evil things, ״וְאֵין אֱלֹהִים עִמָּדִי״. And the proof for this: ״אֲנִי אָמִית וַאֲחַיֶּה וְאֵין מִיָּדִי מַצִּיל״ — and had there been one with Me, he would have saved from My hand, and the man would have been dead-alive, alive-dead, in the blink of an eye. And from this you will understand and gain insight that ״אֵין אֱלֹהִים עִמָּדִי״." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Pesaḥim 68a) derived from here the resurrection of the dead from the Torah, as it says: ״אֲנִי אָמִית וַאֲחַיֶּה״. One might think that death is in one and life in another — Scripture teaches: ״מָחַצְתִּי וַאֲנִי אֶרְפָּא״ — just as 'I wound' and healing are in one, so too death and life are in one. And a support for their words is that He placed death before life, for every person is alive before he dies; rather, He is speaking of the life that comes after death. A second proof, as it says: ״אֲנִי אָמִית וַאֲחַיֶּה״ — for the word 'אֲנִי' stands over both of them, whereas in 'מָחַצְתִּי' He inserted 'אֲנִי' in the middle: ״מָחַצְתִּי וַאֲנִי אֶרְפָּא״, because with death and life there is room to err and to say, death in one and life in another; therefore He did not insert 'אֲנִי' in the middle, so as not to separate between them. But with wounding and healing there is no room to err, for there is no healing without a wound, and certainly all is in one."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The verse citation 'שם ב ו' refers to 1 Samuel 2:6, continuing the passage from Hannah's prayer beginning at 1 Samuel 2:3."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:14.079005706Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}